<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phocaea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phocaea was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> city on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, near present-day Foça in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its sailors were pioneers of Greek trade in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, founding Massalia (Marseille) around 600 BCE, which in turn founded Emporion. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> attacked around 545 BCE many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> fled west, and after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> some founded Elea in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phocaea.jpg" alt="Phocaea" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_theatre_Fo%C3%A7a525.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phocaea was the northernmost of the Ionian cities, on the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>, near the mouth of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Hermus</a>, now the Gediz. It stood on the peninsula that separates the Gulf of Cyme from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-izmir">Gulf of Smyrna</a>, now İzmir, and had two natural harbours, each with small islands, which allowed a seafaring economy and a strong navy. Surveys show a city that was large for the archaic period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes its walls as several stadia long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Pausanias, Phocians under Athenian leadership founded Phocaea on land given by the Aeolian people of Cyme, and the city entered the Ionian League after accepting the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus</a> as kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to make long sea voyages, to the coasts of the Adriatic, Tyrrhenia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arganthonios">Arganthonios</a>, king of Tartessos, invited them to settle in Spain and, when they declined, gave them money to build a wall around their city.</p><p>Their main colonies lay in the west: Massalia (Marseille), founded about 600 BCE, Emporion in Catalonia, dated to 575 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and Rhoda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. They probably also helped to settle Amisos and Lampsacus and traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> were among the earliest users of coined money, which they first made in electrum.</p><p>The city stayed independent until the Lydian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, and then passed with the rest of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> to the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>. Rather than submit, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> abandoned it. Some may have fled to Chios or to the western colonies, and many founded Elea around 540 BCE. Phocaea joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> in 500 BCE, and a Phocaean named Dionysius commanded the Ionian fleet at Lade in 494 BCE, though the city supplied only three ships. It later paid two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> in tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> and rebelled with the rest of Ionia in 412 BCE.</p><p>Under Seleucid and then Attalid rule, and in the Roman period, Phocaea made the pottery known as Phocaean red slip. The Genoese ambassador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-zaccaria">Benedetto Zaccaria</a> received the town as a hereditary lordship, and his family grew rich on its alum mines. It remained a Genoese colony until the Turks took it in 1455.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phocaea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alum from <b>Phocaea</b> and mastic from Chios brought large profits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Greeks from <b>Phocaea</b> founded Massalia about 600 BCE, and Greek wine, bronze vessels and painted pottery travelled up the Rhône to the Hallstatt princes, while Etruscan beaked flagons crossed the Alps to the first La Tène nobles; about 325 BCE Pytheas of Massalia sailed to Britain and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 540 BCE <b>Phocaean Greeks</b>, who had fled their city in Asia Minor before the Persians and then lost much of their fleet at the battle of Alalia off Corsica, founded Elea (later Roman Velia) on a headland south of Poseidonia with help from Rhegion.</blockquote><a href="/state/elea">Elea</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 575 BCE <b>Phocaean Greeks</b> from Massalia (Marseille) set up a trading post on a small island at the mouth of the Fluvià, now Sant Martí d'Empúries.</blockquote><a href="/state/emporion">Emporion</a></li><li><blockquote>Refugees arrived after <b>Phocaea</b> fell to Persia in 545 BCE, and Massalia planted daughter towns such as Nikaia (Nice), Antipolis (Antibes) and Emporion in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a></li></ul></section>
